Solutions Archictect Sent the following to the client to propose the general project configuration and approach and to also ask some definition questions:
1.   Power Apps Code Apps – The proposed Framework
  Power Apps Code Apps (Generally Available from February 2026) lets us build line-of-business apps with standard React + TypeScript and the normal developer toolchain (Vite, npm, Git), while still deploying onto the Power Platform — so the app automatically
  inherits Entra single sign-on, Dataverse data, governance, security roles, and per-user licensing exactly like any traditional Power App.
The benefit is the best of both worlds: modern code-first productivity (component reuse, source control, automated testing, fast iteration on complex UIs) on top of Microsoft-managed infrastructure that already works for governing, monitoring, and auditing — no separate
  hosting, no separate auth, no DBA work, no new compliance surface.

Power Apps Code Apps is part of Power Platform, built by the same Microsoft team that owns Power Apps, Power Automate, and Dataverse. The SDK we're using (@microsoft/power-apps) is published by Microsoft on npm, and the deployment CLI (pac code) is part of the official Power Platform CLI. There's no third party or open-source community project in the trust chain — Microsoft owns the whole stack from the React SDK down to the runtime.

It automatically inherits Microsoft's existing certifications: SOC 1, SOC 2 Type II, ISO 27001/27018, HIPAA BAA, FedRAMP, GDPR, etc. Same data, same auth, same audit trail as any Power App they may already be running — it's not a new surface area for
the compliance team to certify.
  2. What we need to do this properly
  Environment:
-	One non-Developer Dataverse environment, preferably two (Dev + Prod) or three (Dev + Test + Prod) for proper ALM.  Developer environments are single-user by design — they can't host a multi-user app.
-	Options, in order of cost/effort:
o	Sandbox + Production environments (recommended for any business-critical app) — requires Power Platform capacity assigned by your global/tenant admin.
o	Default environment with Dataverse provisioned — cheapest, but Default has no isolation and isn't recommended for anything beyond proof-of-concept.
-	Confirm the region (currently Australia in the prototype). This determines data residency; needs to match your compliance requirements.

  Licenses we will need for the proposed solution:
  Each end user who runs the app needs one of:
-	Power Apps Premium (~$20 USD/user/month) — the standard per-user license. all-you-can-eat seat for that user across every Code App and premium Canvas/Model-driven app in your entire tenant. Flat Fee, independent of use. 
-	Power Apps Pay-as-you-go (~$10 USD per active user/month, billed via Azure subscription retroactively) — Metered, per-app, per-user-who-actually-showed-up access. Same user using 3 different premium apps in a month = $30. Same user not opening the app at all = $0.
-	Power Apps per-app (~$5 USD/user/app/month) — cheaper if a user only ever uses this one app. Flat $5 fee per, independent of use. 

  Code Apps cannot run on the free Power Apps seeded with Microsoft 365 plan (those only cover canvas apps with standard, non-Dataverse connectors). "Developers/makers also need one of the paid entitlements above (Premium, Pay-as-you-go, or per-app) plus maker
  access to the environment.

  Users, roles, and groups:
-	All requesters and approvers must be added as users to the Dataverse environment with appropriate Dataverse security roles.
-	two custom Dataverse security roles — SoftwareRequest-Requester (Read/Create on Request and Messages, Read on Software) and SoftwareRequest-Approver (Read/Update on Request, Read/Create on Messages, Read on Software).

Security groups options:
-	Option A (recommended): Entra ID security group. Create one group, SoftwareRequest-Approvers. Add approvers to it. The app checks group membership via Microsoft Graph. Anyone not in the group is treated as a requester. No "requesters" group or table needed — every authenticated user defaults to the requester experience. Cleanest model:  group membership is the one thing IT manages.
-	Option B: Dataverse table. Maintain a Requesters / Approvers table in Dataverse listing who's authorized. The app queries it at sign-in. More flexible (you can store extra metadata per user — team, manager, etc.), but yet another   table for someone to maintain. Only worth it if you have data tied to each user beyond just "are they an approver?"
-	Option C: Match the Dataverse security role itself. The app could read which security role(s) the user has and route accordingly — no extra Entra group, no extra table. Single source of truth. Slightly trickier to query from the app but doable.
3. Questions

  Governance and approvals:
-	Who needs to sign off before this goes to production? (Power Platform admin? CoE team? IT security? Privacy/compliance?)
-	Do you have a Centre of Excellence or admin team that gatekeeps new apps?
-	Are there existing DLP (data loss prevention) policies on the tenant we need to check the app against? (We use the Dataverse connector — confirm that's on the allow-list.)

  Lifecycle:
-	Will there be a dev → test → prod promotion process? If yes, we'll set up Power Platform pipelines or GitHub Actions to automate it.
-	Who owns the app long-term? (i.e., who pages when something breaks)

  Notifications:
-	Should the requester get an email or Teams notification when their request is approved/rejected/needs more info?
-	Should approvers be notified of new pending requests? This is normally done with a Power Automate flow on the Dataverse table — we just need to know whether to scope it in.

  Identity, data, and compliance:
-	Where should the env live geographically (data residency)?
-	Do you need Dataverse auditing enabled (full who-changed-what log per record)?
-	Any external auditors or compliance frameworks (SOC 2, ISO 27001, GDPR/HIPAA depending on industry) we need to certify against? Power Platform inherits the relevant Microsoft certifications, but the app's data classification still needs sign-off.

  Onboarding:
-	How will new approvers/requesters be enrolled?
-	If it's via the SoftwareRequest-Approvers Entra group, who manages group membership? (Usually the same person/team who already manages M365 group memberships.)
